# Working-tree line endings are LF, on every platform.
#
# Not a style preference. This repo has tests that assert on source text
# itself -- frontend/tests/portfolio-bucket.test.mts checks that a page.tsx
# contains a specific multi-line snippet, and the expectation is written with
# \n. Under a CRLF checkout, which is what core.autocrlf=true gives you on
# Windows, those tests fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the code,
# and the failure reads exactly like a real one. Note that `git checkout-index
# -f` does not undo it: git considers the normalized content identical and
# leaves the existing CRLF files untouched. `git ls-files --eol` is what shows
# the truth (i/lf w/crlf).
#
# Pinning eol=lf here means a fresh clone passes on any platform, instead of
# every developer on Windows rediscovering this and configuring git by hand.
# Existing checkouts are not rewritten by adding this file -- a working tree
# that is already CRLF needs one conversion pass.
* text=auto eol=lf

# text=auto already detects binary content; these are explicit so that a newly
# added asset cannot be misdetected and corrupted by newline translation.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
